1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,776
When an AI agent decides to do something, what actually decides whether it is allowed to happen?

2
00:00:09,326 --> 00:00:12,681
That question is behind this whole project.

3
00:00:12,731 --> 00:00:14,107
It's called Atlas.

4
00:00:17,157 --> 00:00:19,750
Atlas looks like an ordinary assistant.

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,088
You type a request, and it can use tools: read a project record,

6
00:00:24,138 --> 00:00:28,427
open a document, fetch updates from a plugin, or send a message.

7
00:00:33,477 --> 00:00:36,597
Let's build the flow, one piece at a time.

8
00:00:36,647 --> 00:00:39,466
It starts with you, sending a request.

9
00:00:41,916 --> 00:00:46,969
Say you ask: read the project update, and send a summary to the team.

10
00:00:47,019 --> 00:00:50,742
The AI reads your request, and suggests what to do.

11
00:00:53,992 --> 00:00:58,096
Atlas checks each suggestion before anything runs.

12
00:00:58,146 --> 00:01:01,088
If it's allowed, the action happens.

13
00:01:01,138 --> 00:01:03,913
If it's blocked, the action stops.

14
00:01:07,463 --> 00:01:10,840
Engineers call this a security check on the server.

15
00:01:17,390 --> 00:01:19,897
Reading the project update is allowed.

16
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:23,463
Sending the summary needs your approval, so it waits.

17
00:01:27,313 --> 00:01:30,606
Here, Atlas blocked a plugin that wasn't verified.

18
00:01:36,156 --> 00:01:39,983
Some actions are allowed, but only with a human decision.

19
00:01:40,033 --> 00:01:43,044
Sending a message to the team is one of them.

20
00:01:45,094 --> 00:01:48,701
Right now, the action is waiting, not done.

21
00:01:48,751 --> 00:01:51,167
Let's say no first, then yes.

22
00:01:57,717 --> 00:02:02,144
And remember, a send here only writes to a simulated outbox.

23
00:02:02,194 --> 00:02:05,054
No message ever leaves the application.

24
00:02:10,604 --> 00:02:12,601
What if a check can't run?

25
00:02:12,651 --> 00:02:15,751
Atlas stops safely, and nothing happens.

26
00:02:19,601 --> 00:02:22,440
This was a controlled, simulated failure.

27
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:27,584
It does not prove how the system behaves in a real infrastructure outage.

28
00:02:33,134 --> 00:02:35,612
So, what did Atlas demonstrate?

29
00:02:37,462 --> 00:02:38,917
The AI suggests.

30
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:40,140
Atlas checks.

31
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:43,339
The action is allowed, or blocked.

32
00:02:47,389 --> 00:02:50,485
Every decision is saved in an activity record.

33
00:02:53,735 --> 00:02:56,841
You can try the live demo and read the code on GitHub.

34
00:02:56,891 --> 00:02:58,010
Thanks for watching.
